cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below, together with the preprint that describes the part you use."
type: software
title: "Residual-First Framework: pre-registered residual audits of pulsar-timing and gravitational-wave data"
authors:
  - family-names: Kretski
    given-names: Dimitar
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5108-2243"
    affiliation: >-
      Institute of Metal Science, Equipment and Technologies "Acad. A. Balevski",
      Center for Hydro- and Aerodynamics, Bulgarian Academy of Sciences, Varna, Bulgaria
date-released: 2026-10-16
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Kretski/residual-first-framework"
abstract: >-
  Pre-registered residual-level analyses with explicit calibration and stopping rules.
  Pulsar timing: residual-level cross-pulsar statistics applied to NANOGrav 15-yr and
  EPTA DR2 timing residuals. A sky-scramble-calibrated Hellings–Downs test orthogonalised
  against monopole and dipole patterns gives p = 0.0135 (EPTA DR2full, pre-registered
  primary) and p = 0.0065 (DR2new, pre-registered secondary), recovering the known PTA
  signal; an omnibus cross-correlation statistic and temporal-shift nulls are shown to be
  miscalibrated on real PTA sampling.
  Gravitational waves (Module 1, scanner/gw): a frequentist test of a quadratic-in-frequency
  modification of the gravitational-wave group velocity on public LIGO–Virgo–KAGRA data.
  Three pre-registered versions stopped at their own coverage rules before any on-source
  estimate; a secondary IMRPhenomXPHM-only analysis is consistent with zero, and
  full-chain, model-marginalised belts restore coverage at a large cost in sensitivity.
keywords:
  - pre-registration
  - calibration
  - reproducibility
  - pulsar timing arrays
  - Hellings-Downs correlation
  - NANOGrav
  - EPTA
  - gravitational waves
  - modified dispersion
  - frequentist coverage
  - Neyman construction
